#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/leg-floor -- how much of itself must a test leg actually run?

    tools/leg-floor <leg>         print that leg's floor declaration(s)
    tools/leg-floor --all         print every leg's
    tools/leg-floor --list        the same, with the derivation spelled out
    tools/leg-floor --self-test   check that every derivation reads the tree

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============

`make test` reported

    native     passed      exit 0     287 gtests in 21 binaries
    ALL 6 TEST LEGS PASSED

on a run of a leg that has 116 binaries in it. The leg was genuinely
truncated -- a merge landed underneath it -- and NOTHING ANYWHERE NOTICED,
because nothing anywhere knew how many binaries there were supposed to be.

tools/run-test-legs already refuses the two obvious vacuous greens: a leg
that never started (NOT RUN) and a leg that exited 0 reporting no tests at
all (NO TESTS). What it could not refuse was the one in between -- a leg that
ran a fifth of itself, reported a real, non-zero, correctly-extracted count,
and exited 0. Its floor was ONE. One gtest in one binary out of 116 passed.

So each leg now declares, BEFORE IT RUNS, how much of itself there is. The
declaration goes on stdout in a form tools/run-test-legs enforces:

    [leg-floor] gtest-binaries == 116
    [leg-floor] must-not-compile == 4

and the runner -- the only party that observes what actually happened --
fails the leg when what it counted does not clear that.

THE DIVISION OF LABOUR, WHICH IS THE POINT
==========================================

This script DECLARES and never OBSERVES. It runs before the leg, reads the
tree, and says how big the leg is. It cannot see, and must never be given a
way to see, what the leg then did.

tools/run-test-legs OBSERVES and never DECLARES. It counts what came out of
the leg and compares that against a number it did not choose.

Two parties, and neither can produce a green on its own. A leg that lies
about what it ran cannot: the count is the runner's. A runner reading the
wrong log cannot: the floor line in that log will not match what it counted.
That is what makes this different from a leg printing its own "all 116 ran",
which is the shape of the fixture-summary bug this tree already hit -- a
leg's self-reported "53 checks, 3 failed" went straight into the harness
built to stop unbelievable summaries.

WHY NOT A NUMBER IN A FILE
==========================

Because it rots, and this port has been bitten by that twice: two mutation
rows expired when a tree count moved 599 -> 666, and a doc's pinned figures
were red across an unknown number of merges. A pinned 116 is wrong the next
time somebody adds an ss_cpptest() call, and the fix for a stale pin is to
edit the pin -- which teaches the reflex a floor exists to prevent.

So every number below is COUNTED OUT OF THE TREE at the moment the leg
starts. src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt names its binaries; the manifest in tools/checkers
names its checkers; vitest.config.ts names the globs its files come from;
the managed suite's fact attributes are in its sources. Add a test and the
floor rises on its own. Nobody edits anything.

WHY SOME FLOORS ARE `>=` AND SOME ARE `==`
==========================================

`==` where the tree names the units one-for-one and the leg runs each once:
src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt has one `ss_cpptest*()` call per binary, and ctest runs it once,
so 115 is a truncated run and 117 is a log with somebody else's output in
it. Both are wrong and both are refused.

`>=` where the tree names a LOWER BOUND. An xUnit [Theory] is one method and
any number of cases, so the method count floors the test count without
predicting it. A `>=` floor still catches the failure this exists for -- a
leg that ran a fifth of itself is nowhere near its lower bound -- and it does
not go red the day somebody adds an [InlineData] row.

Every `>=` derivation here is deliberately the CONSERVATIVE count, and each
names where it undercounts. An undercount weakens the floor; a guess that
overcounts breaks the build for no reason and gets the floor deleted. Only
the first of those is recoverable.

WHAT A FAILED DERIVATION DOES
=============================

Exits non-zero, so the `&&` in the leg stops the leg before it runs. That is
deliberate and it is the uncomfortable direction: a leg nobody can size is
exactly the leg that reports a fifth of itself as a pass, so it does not get
to run unmeasured. It never falls back to a number, and it never declares a
floor of 0 -- a floor of 0 cannot fail, and a check that cannot fail is worse
than no check.
"""
import contextlib
import glob
import io
import os
import re
import sys

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
ROOT = os.path.abspath(os.path.join(HERE, '..'))

# The units tools/run-test-legs can observe. A floor naming anything else is
# refused there rather than ignored -- a typo'd unit that is silently skipped
# is a floor that cannot fail.
UNITS = (
    'gtest-binaries', 'gtest-tests', 'must-not-compile',
    'dotnet-tests', 'ui-tests', 'ui-files', 'checks',
)


class CannotDerive(Exception):
    """The tree did not answer. Never fall back to a number; fail the leg."""


def _read(rel):
    path = os.path.join(ROOT, rel)
    try:
        with open(path, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as f:
            return f.read()
    except OSError as exc:
        raise CannotDerive('cannot read %s (%s)' % (rel, exc))


def _uncommented(text, marker='#'):
    """Source with whole-line comments removed.

    Load-bearing, not tidy. src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt discusses its tests in prose at
    length -- gib_verdict_test.cpp is named in three comments and compiled on
    one line -- and tools/checkers found by mutation that a substring search
    over the raw file reads a merely-DISCUSSED test as wired. The same trap
    applies to counting: a commented-out ss_cpptest() call must not raise the
    floor for a binary that will never run.
    """
    return '\n'.join(ln for ln in text.splitlines()
                     if not ln.lstrip().startswith(marker))


def _nonzero(rel, what, n):
    if n <= 0:
        raise CannotDerive(
            'derived a floor of %d %s from %s. A floor of zero cannot fail, '
            'so it is refused rather than declared -- either the derivation '
            'has stopped matching the tree, or the tree really has none left, '
            'and both need a human.' % (n, what, rel))
    return n


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The derivations. Each returns a list of (unit, op, n, how).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE NATIVE MANIFEST. Since Phase 6b the native tests are CMake targets, one
# call per binary in src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt, and tools/build/
# buildcpptests is a thin driver over ctest; before that it was the bash script
# itself (one build_and_run* line per binary), and these patterns were
# `^build_and_run(?:_[a-z0-9_]+)?\s+(\S+)` and `^build_must_not_compile\s+(\S+)`.
NATIVE_MANIFEST = 'src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt'

# Any `ss_cpptest*` helper, not an enumerated list of them. The bash version
# was `(?:_mathlib)?`, and the day build_and_run_engine_headers was added the
# floor did NOT rise with it: the leg ran 135 binaries against a floor of 134
# and reported SHORT RUN -- "the log holds output it did not write". That is
# this file's own stated property failing ("Add a test and the floor rises on
# its own"), and enumerating helper names is what made it possible to break by
# adding one. So nothing but a binary-making helper may start with
# `ss_cpptest`: the flag helpers are ss_test_*_flags for that reason.
#
# A call must START a line (so `function(ss_cpptest name)` in the helper file,
# or a mention inside another call, is not one), CMake command names are
# case-insensitive, and the name may sit on the next line after the `(`.
RE_BUILD_RUN = re.compile(r'^ss_cpptest(?:_[a-z0-9_]+)?\s*\(\s*([A-Za-z0-9_.+-]+)',
                          re.M | re.I)
RE_MUST_NOT = re.compile(r'^ss_must_not_compile\s*\(\s*([A-Za-z0-9_.+-]+)', re.M | re.I)
# A binary moved to the census tier (cmake/SourceSharpTests.cmake ss_census):
# still one ss_cpptest*() call, so still counted above, and then taken out of
# the native leg and put in the census leg -- which together are the manifest.
RE_CENSUS = re.compile(r'^ss_census\s*\(\s*([A-Za-z0-9_.+-]+)\s*\)', re.M | re.I)


def _census_split(src, bins):
    census = RE_CENSUS.findall(src)
    stray = sorted(set(census) - set(bins))
    if stray:
        raise CannotDerive(
            '%s moves to the census tier what it never defines: %s'
            % (NATIVE_MANIFEST, ' '.join(stray)))
    if len(set(census)) != len(census):
        raise CannotDerive('%s calls ss_census() twice for one test' % NATIVE_MANIFEST)
    return [b for b in bins if b not in census], census


def _native_manifest():
    """The manifest with whole-line comments out -- refusing a bracket comment,
    which is the one CMake comment form `_uncommented` cannot see."""
    src = _read(NATIVE_MANIFEST)
    if re.search(r'#\[=*\[', src):
        raise CannotDerive(
            '%s has a bracket comment (#[[ ... ]]); a call inside one would be '
            'counted as a binary that never runs. Use line comments.'
            % NATIVE_MANIFEST)
    return _uncommented(src)


def floor_native():
    """One gtest binary per ss_cpptest*() call in src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt.

    src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt IS the native leg's manifest: every binary
    and must-not-compile check the leg runs is one call there, and ctest runs
    each exactly once. This is the execution half: every binary the manifest
    names must actually have run in this invocation.

    `==` and not `>=`: each call builds one binary and ctest runs it once, and
    each run prints exactly one `[==========] N tests ... ran.` line. Fewer is a
    truncated leg; more means the log holds output this leg did not produce.
    """
    src = _native_manifest()
    bins = RE_BUILD_RUN.findall(src)
    nc = RE_MUST_NOT.findall(src)
    names = bins + nc
    bins, census = _census_split(src, bins)
    if len(set(names)) != len(names):
        dup = sorted({b for b in names if names.count(b) > 1})
        raise CannotDerive(
            '%s names these tests twice: %s. The floor counts calls, so a '
            'duplicate name would demand a run that cannot happen.'
            % (NATIVE_MANIFEST, ' '.join(dup)))
    return [
        ('gtest-binaries', '==',
         _nonzero(NATIVE_MANIFEST, 'binaries', len(bins)),
         '%d ss_cpptest*() calls, less %d ss_census()' % (len(bins), len(census))),
        ('must-not-compile', '==',
         _nonzero(NATIVE_MANIFEST, 'must-not-compile checks', len(nc)),
         '%d ss_must_not_compile() calls' % len(nc)),
    ]


def floor_census():
    """The census tier (make test-full): the ss_cpptest*() binaries the
    manifest moves there with ss_census(), run by buildcpptests --census."""
    src = _native_manifest()
    _, census = _census_split(src, RE_BUILD_RUN.findall(src))
    return [('gtest-binaries', '==',
             _nonzero(NATIVE_MANIFEST, 'census binaries', len(census)),
             '%d ss_census() calls' % len(census))]


RE_CUSTOM_FACT = re.compile(r'class\s+(\w+?)Attribute\s*:\s*FactAttribute')


RE_THEORY = re.compile(r'\[Theory\s*[\](]')
RE_INLINE = re.compile(r'\[InlineData\s*[\](]')


def floor_managed():
    """One dotnet test, at least, per xUnit case the sources spell out.

    The same rule tools/checkers' sweep_managed applies one file at a time: a
    test method is one xUnit will discover, which means [Fact], [Theory], or
    an attribute the suite declares as a FactAttribute subclass. The custom
    ones are FOUND rather than listed -- SandboxMapFact is the only one today
    and naming it here would go stale the way a pinned count does.

    A [Theory] is ONE method and as many cases as it has data rows, so
    counting methods left the floor 73 short of the truth -- and 73 of slack
    is 73 tests that can stop running with nothing to say so. A whole test
    class made non-public (70 tests, no diagnostic anywhere, exactly the shape
    CLAUDE.md warns about) slipped through a method count and does not slip
    through this one. So the rows are counted where they are written down:

        facts + max(InlineData rows, [Theory] methods)

    which is 1,292 against a real 1,332 today. `>=`, and the remaining 40 are
    named: [MemberData]/[ClassData] rows are computed at runtime and cannot be
    counted from source, and a [Fact] inherited from a base class is not
    spelled in the derived file. Both leave the floor BELOW the truth, which
    is the safe direction -- a floor that overshoots breaks the build for no
    reason and gets deleted.

    If those 40 ever matter, the exact number is available from
    `dotnet test --list-tests`, which enumerates every case including theory
    expansion without running any of them. It is not used here because it
    needs the toolchain and a build, and this script must stay hermetic and
    instant: it runs before every leg, including on trees that cannot build.

    WHOLE-LINE // COMMENTS ARE STRIPPED FIRST, for the reason
    the native manifest's are: a commented-out [Fact] is a test that does not
    run, and counting it would raise the floor for a case that cannot happen.
    """
    return _floor_dotnet('src/test/managed', TEST_COMMON)


def floor_managed_server():
    """The server's facts (src/test/server, plan_side_isolation P6): the
    managed rule over that project alone. Its custom fact attributes are
    declared in the linked helpers (TEST_COMMON), so they are read there."""
    return _floor_dotnet('src/test/server', TEST_COMMON)


def floor_managed_client():
    """The client's facts (src/test/client); see floor_managed_server."""
    return _floor_dotnet('src/test/client', TEST_COMMON)


# The fact-less helpers the test projects link (plan_side_isolation P6). They
# hold no case to count -- a fact there would run once per linking project --
# but they declare the custom FactAttribute subclasses the projects use.
TEST_COMMON = 'src/test/common'


def _cs_texts(d):
    root = os.path.join(ROOT, d)
    if not os.path.isdir(root):
        raise CannotDerive('%s is not there' % d)
    texts = []
    for dirpath, dirs, files in os.walk(root):
        dirs[:] = [x for x in dirs if x not in ('obj', 'bin')]
        for fn in sorted(files):
            if fn.endswith('.cs'):
                with open(os.path.join(dirpath, fn), encoding='utf-8',
                          errors='replace') as f:
                    texts.append(_uncommented(f.read(), '//'))
    if not texts:
        raise CannotDerive('%s holds no .cs files' % d)
    return texts


def _floor_dotnet(d, attributes=None):
    texts = _cs_texts(d)

    allowed = {'Fact'}
    for txt in texts + (_cs_texts(attributes) if attributes else []):
        allowed.update(RE_CUSTOM_FACT.findall(txt))
    pat = re.compile(r'\[(?:%s)\s*[\](]' % '|'.join(sorted(allowed)))
    facts = sum(len(pat.findall(t)) for t in texts)
    theories = sum(len(RE_THEORY.findall(t)) for t in texts)
    inline = sum(len(RE_INLINE.findall(t)) for t in texts)
    n = facts + max(inline, theories)
    return [('dotnet-tests', '>=', _nonzero(d, 'xUnit cases', n),
             '%d [%s] + %d theory rows (%d [Theory], %d [InlineData]) '
             'in %d .cs files'
             % (facts, '/'.join(sorted(allowed)), max(inline, theories),
                theories, inline, len(texts)))]


RE_VITEST_INCLUDE = re.compile(r'include\s*:\s*\[(.*?)\]', re.S)


def floor_ui():
    """One vitest file per file vitest's own include globs match.

    The globs are read out of vitest.config.ts rather than written down again
    here, so the leg and its floor cannot disagree about what a test file is.
    A second copy of a pattern is how they always do.
    """
    cfgrel = 'src/webui/vitest.config.ts'
    cfg = _read(cfgrel)
    m = RE_VITEST_INCLUDE.search(cfg)
    if not m:
        raise CannotDerive(
            'no `include:` array in %s. The floor is the set of files vitest '
            'itself would collect, so without the globs there is nothing to '
            'count.' % cfgrel)
    globs = re.findall(r'''['"]([^'"]+)['"]''', m.group(1))
    if not globs:
        raise CannotDerive('the `include:` array in %s is empty' % cfgrel)
    webui = os.path.dirname(os.path.join(ROOT, cfgrel))
    found = set()
    for g in globs:
        found.update(glob.glob(os.path.join(webui, g), recursive=True))
    return [('ui-files', '==', _nonzero(cfgrel, 'test files', len(found)),
             '%d files matching %s' % (len(found), ' '.join(globs)))]


# The check-emitting call site in each self-test, which is what its
# "<n> checks, <m> failed" line counts. Anchored at the start of a line so a
# mention inside a comment or a string does not raise the floor.
RE_ST_OK = r'^[ \t]*_st_ok '
RE_CK = r'^[ \t]*ck\('


def _count_sites(rel, pattern):
    return len(re.findall(pattern, _read(rel), re.M))


def floor_runner():
    """The harness's own two self-tests, counted by their check call sites.

    The `runner` leg is `tools/run-test-legs --self-test` plus this script's
    --self-test, and both report through the generic "<n> checks, <m> failed"
    contract, so the leg's count is the sum.

    `>=` because a self-test may add a check that is not an _st_ok/ck call --
    tools/checkers has exactly one, its fixture-leak guard. Undercounting by
    one or two still catches what matters here, which is one of the two halves
    not running at all.
    """
    a = _count_sites('tools/run-test-legs', RE_ST_OK)
    b = _count_sites('tools/leg-floor', RE_CK)
    return [('checks', '>=',
             _nonzero('tools/run-test-legs', 'checks', a + b),
             '%d _st_ok in run-test-legs + %d ck( in leg-floor' % (a, b))]


# THE TREE CHECKS (tools/checks.tsv, run by tools/run-checks): one manifest
# row per check, and run-checks prints exactly one `<n> checks, <m> failed`
# line for the rows of the tier it ran (a check's own summary is defused in
# its output). So the floor is the row count, and `==`: fewer is a truncated
# leg, more is output this leg did not write.
CHECKS_MANIFEST = 'tools/checks.tsv'


def _check_rows(tier):
    n = 0
    names = []
    for line in _read(CHECKS_MANIFEST).splitlines():
        if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith('#'):
            continue
        parts = line.split('\t')
        if len(parts) != 3:
            raise CannotDerive('%s: %r is not name<TAB>tier<TAB>command'
                               % (CHECKS_MANIFEST, line))
        names.append(parts[0].strip())
        if parts[1].strip() == tier:
            n += 1
    if len(set(names)) != len(names):
        raise CannotDerive('%s names a check twice' % CHECKS_MANIFEST)
    return [('checks', '==', _nonzero(CHECKS_MANIFEST, '%s checks' % tier, n),
             '%d %s rows in %s' % (n, tier, CHECKS_MANIFEST))]


def floor_checks():
    """The routine tier of tools/checks.tsv (make test)."""
    return _check_rows('routine')


def floor_checks_full():
    """The full tier of tools/checks.tsv (make test-full adds it)."""
    return _check_rows('full')


# A row of either instrument's committed file: not blank, not a comment, and
# carrying the column count that file's reader demands. Counting every
# non-comment line instead would let a stray fragment raise the floor for a
# check nothing runs.
def _tsv_rows(rel, columns):
    n = 0
    for line in _read(rel).splitlines():
        if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith('#'):
            continue
        if len(line.split('\t')) == columns:
            n += 1
    return n


def floor_entity():
    """plan_entity.md step 8c's leg: the census, the three gates, the budget.

    THREE POPULATIONS, EACH COUNTED WHERE IT IS WRITTEN DOWN, so the floor
    rises on its own the way every other one here does:

      * docs/entity-ratchet.tsv  -- one gated (or reconciliation) row of
        `tools/entity-gates --tsv`, one check each. A row added to the gates
        and baselined raises this by one.
      * tools/live-gate-budget.tsv -- one live-gate stage, one check each. A
        stage added to tools/live-gate raises it by one, because the leg fails
        until that stage has a budget row.
      * tools/entity-ratchet's own --self-test, by its ck() call sites, for the
        reason floor_runner counts this file's: the leg runs the fixtures
        first, and a fixture suite that stopped running is exactly how a
        comparison that can no longer fail goes unnoticed.

    `>=`, and the undercount is NAMED rather than left as slack: the leg also
    runs five checks that are a property of the tool rather than of any row --
    the census diff, and the two set checks each of the gate and budget halves
    ("every baselined row was measured" and its converse, "every live-gate
    stage has a budget row" and its converse). 85 against a real 90 today.
    Undercounting weakens the floor; overcounting breaks the build for no
    reason and gets the floor deleted, and only the first is recoverable.
    """
    rows = _tsv_rows('docs/entity-ratchet.tsv', 5)
    stages = _tsv_rows('tools/live-gate-budget.tsv', 10)
    fixtures = _count_sites('tools/entity-ratchet', RE_CK)
    # EACH POPULATION IS REFUSED AT ZERO, not just their sum. Two of the three
    # could empty out and the sum would still be a plausible non-zero number,
    # and a floor the leg clears while measuring nothing is the shape this file
    # exists to remove. _nonzero on the total alone would not have caught it.
    for what, count, where in (('ratchet rows', rows, 'docs/entity-ratchet.tsv'),
                               ('budget stages', stages, 'tools/live-gate-budget.tsv'),
                               ('ck( sites', fixtures, 'tools/entity-ratchet')):
        _nonzero(where, what, count)
    n = rows + stages + fixtures
    return [('checks', '>=',
             _nonzero('docs/entity-ratchet.tsv', 'checks', n),
             '%d ratchet rows + %d budget stages + %d ck( in entity-ratchet'
             % (rows, stages, fixtures))]


RE_FIXTURE_TREE = re.compile(r'^FIXTURE_TREE_EXPECT = \{(.*?)^\}', re.S | re.M)


RE_MANIFEST = re.compile(r'^MANIFEST = \[(.*?)^\]', re.S | re.M)

# THE ORACLE LEG (src/test/oracle): ports checked against upstream Valve code
# read from the pristine clone at valve-sdk.lock. Two halves, each sized the
# way its kind is sized elsewhere here: the managed project's xUnit cases, and
# one binary per ss_cpptest*() call in the oracle CMakeLists (the same helpers;
# tools/build/buildcpptests --oracle runs them and prints `==> <name>`).
ORACLE_MANAGED = 'src/test/oracle/managed'
ORACLE_NATIVE = 'src/test/oracle/native/CMakeLists.txt'


def _oracle_manifest():
    src = _read(ORACLE_NATIVE)
    if re.search(r'#\[=*\[', src):
        raise CannotDerive('%s has a bracket comment' % ORACLE_NATIVE)
    return _uncommented(src)


def floor_oracle():
    """The oracle project's xUnit cases, and its native binaries."""
    bins = RE_BUILD_RUN.findall(_oracle_manifest())
    if len(set(bins)) != len(bins):
        raise CannotDerive('%s names a binary twice' % ORACLE_NATIVE)
    return _floor_dotnet(ORACLE_MANAGED) + [
        ('gtest-binaries', '==',
         _nonzero(ORACLE_NATIVE, 'oracle binaries', len(bins)),
         '%d ss_cpptest*() calls' % len(bins))]


def floor_oracle_managed():
    """The oracle leg's managed half alone: `make test` runs the two halves as
    separate legs, so each can share a group with the leg whose tool it uses
    (tools/run-test-legs: dotnet with dotnet, ninja with ninja)."""
    return _floor_dotnet(ORACLE_MANAGED)


def floor_descent():
    """The TF2Descent mod's facts (src/tf2_descent/tests): the same counting rule as
    the managed leg, over that project alone."""
    return _floor_dotnet('src/tf2_descent/tests')


def floor_tf2():
    """The TF2 mod's facts (src/tf2/tests): the same counting rule as
    the managed leg, over that project alone."""
    return _floor_dotnet('src/tf2/tests')


def floor_oracle_native():
    """The oracle leg's native half alone; see floor_oracle_managed."""
    return [f for f in floor_oracle() if f[0] == 'gtest-binaries']


def roster_oracle():
    return [('gtest-binaries', n)
            for n in RE_BUILD_RUN.findall(_oracle_manifest())]


def roster_native():
    """Not how many binaries, but WHICH -- by name, one line each.

    A count says "95 never ran". A roster says which 95.
    src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt already names every binary, and
    tools/build/buildcpptests prints `==> <name>` over each one's output, so
    the names it declared and the names that appear in the log
    can be compared directly -- and the leg's failure names the binaries that
    were missing instead of a subtraction.

    This is the honest form of the floor, and the count is kept alongside it
    rather than replaced: the roster proves each binary was REACHED, and the
    count proves each one RAN. A binary whose `==>` printed and whose gtest
    summary never did is caught by the count and by nothing else.
    """
    src = _native_manifest()
    bins, _ = _census_split(src, RE_BUILD_RUN.findall(src))
    return ([('gtest-binaries', n) for n in bins]
            + [('must-not-compile', n) for n in RE_MUST_NOT.findall(src)])


def roster_census():
    src = _native_manifest()
    _, census = _census_split(src, RE_BUILD_RUN.findall(src))
    return [('gtest-binaries', n) for n in census]


# THE TIERS (Makefile TEST_LEGS / TEST_FULL_LEGS). `make test` runs checks,
# managed, managed-server, managed-client, tf2_descent, native, entity and ui;
# `make test-full` adds runner, checks-full, oracle-managed, oracle-native and
# census. Every leg here belongs to exactly one tier, so each tier's size is the
# sum of its legs' floors (`tools/leg-floor --list`). native and census split
# src/test/native/CMakeLists.txt's binaries by ss_census(), checks and
# checks-full split tools/checks.tsv by its tier column: in both, the two legs
# together are the whole manifest. `oracle` is the two oracle legs' sum, for
# `make oracle-test`; no tier runs it as a leg.
LEGS = {
    'entity': floor_entity,
    'runner': floor_runner,
    'checks': floor_checks,
    'checks-full': floor_checks_full,
    'managed': floor_managed,
    'managed-server': floor_managed_server,
    'managed-client': floor_managed_client,
    'tf2_descent': floor_descent,
    'tf2': floor_tf2,
    'native': floor_native,
    'census': floor_census,
    'oracle': floor_oracle,
    'oracle-managed': floor_oracle_managed,
    'oracle-native': floor_oracle_native,
    'ui': floor_ui,
}

# THE FAST AND SLOW TIERS (make test-fast / make test-slow; tools/test-tiers).
# Each managed leg and the native leg has a -fast variant, which runs the leg
# less the slow tier tools/test-tiers.tsv lists, and a -slow variant for every
# leg the list names something in, which runs exactly that. The fast floor is
# the full leg's less the cases the list moved out; the slow floor is one case
# per entry, so a stale entry that names nothing makes its leg run short.
# Neither is a gate: `make test` still runs every leg whole.
def _test_tiers():
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
        'test_tiers', os.path.join(HERE, 'test-tiers'))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader('test_tiers', loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


def _tier_rows(leg):
    tiers = _test_tiers()
    try:
        return tiers, tiers.entries(tiers.load(), leg)
    except tiers.TierError as exc:
        raise CannotDerive(str(exc))


def _managed_tier(leg, tier):
    tiers, rows = _tier_rows(leg)
    (unit, _op, n, how), = LEGS[leg]()
    if tier == 'fast':
        cut = sum(r.cases for r in rows)
        return [(unit, '>=', _nonzero(tiers.LIST, '%s-fast cases' % leg, n - cut),
                 '%s, less %d cases in %d slow entries' % (how, cut, len(rows)))]
    return [(unit, '>=', _nonzero(tiers.LIST, '%s slow entries' % leg, len(rows)),
             'one case per entry in %s' % tiers.LIST)]


def _native_tier_roster(tier):
    tiers, rows = _tier_rows('native')
    slow = {r.name for r in rows}
    full = roster_native()
    unknown = sorted(slow - {name for _unit, name in full})
    if unknown:
        raise CannotDerive('%s names native tests the manifest does not: %s'
                           % (tiers.LIST, ' '.join(unknown)))
    return [(u, name) for u, name in full if (name in slow) == (tier == 'slow')]


def _native_tier(tier):
    names = _native_tier_roster(tier)
    out = []
    for unit in ('gtest-binaries', 'must-not-compile'):
        n = sum(1 for u, _name in names if u == unit)
        if n or tier == 'fast':
            out.append((unit, '==', _nonzero(NATIVE_MANIFEST, 'native-%s %s' % (tier, unit), n),
                        '%d of the native leg\'s %s, by tools/test-tiers.tsv' % (n, unit)))
    if not out:
        raise CannotDerive('the native leg has no %s tests' % tier)
    return out


def _register_tiers():
    """-fast for every tiered leg; -slow for those the list names something
    in. A list that does not parse registers the -fast legs anyway, which then
    refuse with the reason."""
    managed = ('managed', 'managed-server', 'managed-client', 'tf2_descent', 'tf2')
    try:
        tiers = _test_tiers()
        rows = tiers.load()
    except Exception:
        rows = None
    for leg in managed:
        LEGS[leg + '-fast'] = (lambda leg=leg: _managed_tier(leg, 'fast'))
        if rows and any(r.leg == leg for r in rows):
            LEGS[leg + '-slow'] = (lambda leg=leg: _managed_tier(leg, 'slow'))
    LEGS['native-fast'] = lambda: _native_tier('fast')
    ROSTERS['native-fast'] = lambda: _native_tier_roster('fast')
    if rows and any(r.leg == 'native' for r in rows):
        LEGS['native-slow'] = lambda: _native_tier('slow')
        ROSTERS['native-slow'] = lambda: _native_tier_roster('slow')


# Legs whose units are NAMED things rather than a population. Only the native
# leg has one today: it is the only leg whose manifest is a list of names the
# log prints back. tools/run-test-legs refuses a roster on any unit it has no
# way to spot in a log, rather than ignoring it.
ROSTERS = {
    'native': roster_native,
    'census': roster_census,
    'oracle': roster_oracle,
    'oracle-native': roster_oracle,
}
_register_tiers()


def declare(leg, explain=False, out=None):
    out = out or sys.stdout
    for unit, op, n, how in LEGS[leg]():
        if unit not in UNITS:
            raise CannotDerive(
                '%s is not a unit tools/run-test-legs can observe (%s)'
                % (unit, ' '.join(UNITS)))
        if explain:
            out.write('%-10s [leg-floor] %-16s %s %-6d  %s\n'
                      % (leg, unit, op, n, how))
        else:
            out.write('[leg-floor] %s %s %d\n' % (unit, op, n))
    if leg in ROSTERS:
        names = ROSTERS[leg]()
        if not names:
            raise CannotDerive(
                'the %s leg declares a roster and the roster is empty. An '
                'empty roster is satisfied by any run at all.' % leg)
        for unit, name in names:
            if explain:
                out.write('%-10s [leg-roster] %-16s %s\n' % (leg, unit, name))
            else:
                out.write('[leg-roster] %s %s\n' % (unit, name))


def main(argv):
    if not argv or argv[0] in ('-h', '--help'):
        sys.stderr.write(__doc__.split('\n\n')[0].strip() + '\n')
        return 2
    if argv[0] in ('--all', '--list'):
        rc = 0
        for leg in sorted(LEGS):
            try:
                declare(leg, argv[0] == '--list')
            except CannotDerive as exc:
                sys.stderr.write('leg-floor: %s: %s\n' % (leg, exc))
                rc = 1
        return rc
    leg = argv[0]
    if leg not in LEGS:
        sys.stderr.write('leg-floor: no such leg %r (have: %s)\n'
                         % (leg, ' '.join(sorted(LEGS))))
        return 2
    try:
        declare(leg)
    except CannotDerive as exc:
        sys.stderr.write(
            'leg-floor: cannot derive the %s leg\'s floor: %s\n'
            '           The leg is NOT run. A leg nobody can size is exactly '
            'the leg\n'
            '           that reports a fifth of itself as a pass.\n'
            % (leg, exc))
        return 1
    return 0


# ===========================================================================
# Self-test.
#
# The question it has to answer is not "does this print a number" -- an
# instrument with no tree under it answered 0 to everything in this port, and
# three of its five mutation controls passed on 0 -> 0. It is:
#
#   1. does each derivation READ THE TREE?  Proved by mutation: change the
#      fixture, require the number to move, and require it to move by the
#      right amount.
#   2. does a tree with nothing in it FAIL rather than declare 0?
#   3. is the derivation still pointed at the REAL sources?  A fixture is a
#      stand-in and stand-ins drift; the last block asserts the production
#      tree answers too, and answers something bigger than a toy.
# ===========================================================================
def self_test():
    import shutil
    import tempfile

    checks = failed = 0
    transcripts = []

    def quiet(fn, *a, **kw):
        """Run a derivation with its output CAPTURED.

        Not tidiness. tools/run-test-legs reads `[leg-floor] ...` lines out of
        a leg's log and enforces them, and the fixture derivations below emit
        exactly that line with deliberately wrong numbers in it. Left on
        stdout they would become this leg's floor -- a self-test dictating the
        gate it is testing. tools/checkers hit the same shape from the other
        end and its comment is the precedent: a leg whose summary cannot be
        believed is what the harness exists to prevent.
        """
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
            rv = fn(*a, **kw)
        transcripts.append(buf.getvalue())
        return rv

    def ck(label, cond, detail=''):
        nonlocal checks, failed
        checks += 1
        print('  %-5s %s%s' % ('ok' if cond else 'FAIL', label,
                               ('   -- ' + detail) if detail else ''))
        if not cond:
            failed += 1

    def n_of(fn, unit='ignored'):
        """The number one derivation produced, or None if it refused."""
        try:
            rows = fn()
        except CannotDerive:
            return None
        for u, _op, n, _how in rows:
            if unit == 'ignored' or u == unit:
                return n
        return None

    print('==> tools/leg-floor self-test')

    global ROOT
    saved_root = ROOT
    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='leg-floor-selftest.')
    try:
        ROOT = tmp

        def put(rel, body):
            p = os.path.join(tmp, rel)
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
            with open(p, 'w') as f:
                f.write(body)

        # -- tree checks ---------------------------------------------------
        put(CHECKS_MANIFEST,
            '# a comment\na\troutine\ttools/a\nb\troutine\ttools/b\n'
            'c\tfull\ttools/c\n')
        ck('checks counts the routine rows', n_of(floor_checks, 'checks') == 2,
           'got %s, want 2' % n_of(floor_checks, 'checks'))
        ck('checks-full counts the full rows', n_of(floor_checks_full, 'checks') == 1,
           'got %s, want 1' % n_of(floor_checks_full, 'checks'))
        with open(os.path.join(tmp, CHECKS_MANIFEST), 'a') as f:
            f.write('d\troutine\ttools/d\n')
        ck('adding a routine row raises the checks floor by one',
           n_of(floor_checks, 'checks') == 3, 'got %s' % n_of(floor_checks, 'checks'))
        put(CHECKS_MANIFEST, 'a\tfull\ttools/a\n')
        ck('a tier with no rows REFUSES rather than declaring 0',
           n_of(floor_checks, 'checks') is None)
        put(CHECKS_MANIFEST, 'a\troutine\ttools/a\na\tfull\ttools/a\n')
        ck('a check named twice is refused', n_of(floor_checks, 'checks') is None)
        put(CHECKS_MANIFEST, 'a\troutine\n')
        ck('a malformed row is refused', n_of(floor_checks, 'checks') is None)

        # -- native ------------------------------------------------------
        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST,
            '# a comment naming ghost_test.cpp at length\n'
            '# ss_cpptest(ghost SOURCES sourcesharp/test/ghost_test.cpp)\n'
            'ss_cpptest(alpha\n    SOURCES sourcesharp/test/alpha_test.cpp)\n'
            'ss_cpptest_mathlib(beta SOURCES sourcesharp/test/beta_test.cpp)\n'
            'ss_must_not_compile(gamma EXPECT "error: no" SOURCES g.cpp)\n'
            'ss_test_compile_flags(ss_cpptest_dtobjs)\n')
        ck('native counts ss_cpptest AND ss_cpptest_mathlib',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') == 2,
           'got %s, want 2' % n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries'))
        ck('native counts ss_must_not_compile separately',
           n_of(floor_native, 'must-not-compile') == 1,
           'got %s, want 1' % n_of(floor_native, 'must-not-compile'))
        ck('an ss_cpptest call that is only a COMMENT does not count',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') == 2,
           'the fixture comments out a third binary')

        before = n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')
        with open(os.path.join(tmp, NATIVE_MANIFEST), 'a') as f:
            f.write('ss_cpptest(delta SOURCES sourcesharp/test/delta_test.cpp)\n')
        ck('adding a binary raises the native floor by one',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') == before + 1,
           '%s -> %s' % (before, n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')))

        # A helper this file has never heard of. The pattern used to enumerate
        # them -- `(?:_mathlib)?` -- and the day build_and_run_engine_headers
        # landed the floor did not rise with it: 135 binaries ran against a
        # floor of 134 and the leg reported SHORT RUN, "the log holds output it
        # did not write". The floor's whole claim is that adding a test raises
        # it on its own, so a helper name it must be taught is a defect in the
        # floor rather than in the recipe.
        before = n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')
        with open(os.path.join(tmp, NATIVE_MANIFEST), 'a') as f:
            f.write('ss_cpptest_never_seen_before(eps '
                    'SOURCES sourcesharp/test/eps_test.cpp)\n')
        ck('an ss_cpptest helper this file does not enumerate still counts',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') == before + 1,
           '%s -> %s' % (before, n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')))

        # And the definition of such a helper is not a binary: `function(ss_cpptest_x
        # {` has a `(` where the pattern needs whitespace, which is the only
        # thing keeping the generalised pattern from counting every helper twice.
        before = n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')
        with open(os.path.join(tmp, NATIVE_MANIFEST), 'a') as f:
            f.write('function(ss_cpptest_never_seen_before name)\n'
                    '  _ss_cpptest(PLAIN ${name} ${ARGN})\nendfunction()\n')
        ck('a helper DEFINITION line is not counted as a binary',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') == before,
           '%s -> %s' % (before, n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')))

        # The census tier: ss_census() moves a binary from the native leg to
        # the census leg, and the two together are every ss_cpptest*() call.
        before = n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')
        with open(os.path.join(tmp, NATIVE_MANIFEST), 'a') as f:
            f.write('ss_census(alpha)\n')
        ck('ss_census() takes a binary out of the native leg',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') == before - 1,
           '%s -> %s' % (before, n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries')))
        ck('and puts it in the census leg', n_of(floor_census, 'gtest-binaries') == 1,
           'got %s' % n_of(floor_census, 'gtest-binaries'))
        ck('the census roster names it, the native roster does not',
           roster_census() == [('gtest-binaries', 'alpha')]
           and ('gtest-binaries', 'alpha') not in roster_native())
        with open(os.path.join(tmp, NATIVE_MANIFEST), 'a') as f:
            f.write('ss_census(nosuch)\n')
        ck('ss_census() of a test the manifest never defines is refused',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') is None
           and n_of(floor_census, 'gtest-binaries') is None)

        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST, '# nothing but prose\n')
        ck('a recipe with no binaries REFUSES rather than declaring 0',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') is None)

        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST,
            'ss_cpptest(twice SOURCES a.cpp)\nss_cpptest(twice SOURCES b.cpp)\n'
            'ss_must_not_compile(g EXPECT "e" SOURCES g.cpp)\n')
        ck('a duplicate binary name is refused, not counted twice',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') is None)

        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST,
            'ss_cpptest(same SOURCES a.cpp)\n'
            'ss_must_not_compile(same EXPECT "e" SOURCES a.cpp)\n')
        ck('a binary and a must-not-compile check with one name are refused '
           '(ctest cannot hold both)',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') is None)

        # CMake command names are case-insensitive, and a call may put the
        # name on the line after its `(`. Both still build a binary, so both
        # must raise the floor -- a spelling the pattern missed is a binary
        # that runs with no floor under it.
        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST,
            'SS_CPPTEST(upper SOURCES a.cpp)\n'
            'ss_cpptest_tier1 (\n    spaced\n    SOURCES b.cpp)\n'
            'ss_must_not_compile(g EXPECT "e" SOURCES g.cpp)\n')
        ck('an upper-case call and a name on the next line are both counted',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') == 2,
           'got %s, want 2' % n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries'))

        # A bracket comment is the one CMake comment whole-line stripping
        # cannot see: a call inside it would be declared and never run.
        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST,
            'ss_cpptest(alpha SOURCES a.cpp)\n#[[\nss_cpptest(hidden SOURCES h.cpp)\n]]\n'
            'ss_must_not_compile(g EXPECT "e" SOURCES g.cpp)\n')
        ck('a bracket comment in the manifest is REFUSED, not half-read',
           n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries') is None)

        # -- managed -----------------------------------------------------
        # The linked helpers (TEST_COMMON) hold no fact; the projects read
        # their custom attributes there.
        put('src/test/common/Helpers.cs', 'class H {}\n')
        put('src/test/managed/ATests.cs',
            'class A { [Fact] public void X(){} [Theory] public void Y(){}\n'
            '  [Fact(Skip="s")] public void Z(){} }\n')
        ck('managed counts [Fact], [Fact(...)] and [Theory]',
           n_of(floor_managed) == 3, 'got %s, want 3' % n_of(floor_managed))

        put('src/test/common/MapFactAttribute.cs',
            'public sealed class SandboxMapFactAttribute : FactAttribute {}\n')
        put('src/test/managed/BTests.cs',
            'class B { [SandboxMapFact] public void W(){} }\n')
        ck('a FactAttribute SUBCLASS is discovered, not listed',
           n_of(floor_managed) == 4, 'got %s, want 4' % n_of(floor_managed))

        put('src/test/server/STests.cs',
            'class S { [SandboxMapFact] public void W(){} [Fact] public void V(){} }\n')
        put('src/test/client/CTests.cs', 'class C { [Fact] public void V(){} }\n')
        ck("each side's project is floored alone, with the shared attributes",
           (n_of(floor_managed_server), n_of(floor_managed_client), n_of(floor_managed))
           == (2, 1, 4), 'got %s' % ((n_of(floor_managed_server),
                                     n_of(floor_managed_client), n_of(floor_managed)),))

        # A [Theory] is one method and as many cases as it has rows. Counting
        # methods left 73 of slack, and a whole test class going undiscovered
        # (70 tests) fitted inside it.
        put('src/test/managed/ATests.cs',
            'class A { [Fact] public void X(){}\n'
            '  [Theory] [InlineData(1)] [InlineData(2)] [InlineData(3)]\n'
            '  public void Y(int i){} }\n')
        ck('a [Theory] counts its InlineData rows, not itself',
           n_of(floor_managed) == 5, 'got %s, want 1 fact + 3 rows + 1 '
           'SandboxMapFact' % n_of(floor_managed))

        put('src/test/managed/ATests.cs',
            'class A { [Fact] public void X(){}\n'
            '  // [Fact] public void Disabled(){}\n'
            '  [Theory] public void Y(){} }\n')
        ck('a commented-out [Fact] does not raise the floor',
           n_of(floor_managed) == 3, 'got %s, want 1 fact + 1 bare theory + 1 '
           'SandboxMapFact' % n_of(floor_managed))

        put('src/test/managed/ATests.cs',
            'class A { public void X(){} }\n')
        put('src/test/managed/BTests.cs',
            'class B { public void W(){} }\n')
        ck('a suite with no fact methods REFUSES rather than declaring 0',
           n_of(floor_managed) is None)

        # -- ui ----------------------------------------------------------
        put('src/webui/vitest.config.ts',
            "export default { test: { include: "
            "['src/**/*.test.ts', 'src/**/*.test.tsx'] } };\n")
        put('src/webui/src/a.test.ts', '')
        put('src/webui/src/deep/b.test.tsx', '')
        put('src/webui/src/deep/notatest.ts', '')
        ck('ui counts the files vitest\'s own globs match, recursively',
           n_of(floor_ui) == 2, 'got %s, want 2' % n_of(floor_ui))

        put('src/webui/vitest.config.ts',
            "export default { test: { include: ['src/**/*.test.ts'] } };\n")
        ck('narrowing the config\'s globs narrows the floor',
           n_of(floor_ui) == 1, 'got %s, want 1' % n_of(floor_ui))

        put('src/webui/vitest.config.ts',
            "export default { test: { environment: 'jsdom' } };\n")
        ck('a config with no include array REFUSES rather than declaring 0',
           n_of(floor_ui) is None)

        # -- the "<n> checks" legs ---------------------------------------
        put('tools/run-test-legs',
            '    _st_ok a "x" ok\n    _st_ok b "y" ok\n'
            '# _st_ok c "commented out" ok\n')
        put('tools/leg-floor', '        ck("one", True)\n')
        ck('runner sums both self-tests\' check call sites',
           n_of(floor_runner) == 3, 'got %s, want 3' % n_of(floor_runner))
        ck('a check site that is only a COMMENT does not count',
           n_of(floor_runner) == 3)

        put('tools/run-test-legs', '# no checks here at all\n')
        put('tools/leg-floor', '# nor here\n')
        ck('a self-test with no checks REFUSES rather than declaring 0',
           n_of(floor_runner) is None)

        # -- entity ------------------------------------------------------
        put('docs/entity-ratchet.tsv',
            '# gate\trole\tmetric\tserver\tclient\n'
            '1\tgate\tcstyle\t3\t4\n'
            '3\tgate\tEHANDLE\t5\t6\n'
            'a stray fragment with no columns at all\n')
        put('tools/live-gate-budget.tsv',
            '# columns:\n'
            'join\ttotal\t1\t2\t3\t4\t5\t6\t7\t8\n')
        put('tools/entity-ratchet',
            '    ck("one", True)\n    ck("two", True)\n'
            '# ck("commented out", True)\n')
        ck('entity sums ratchet rows, budget stages and its own fixture checks',
           n_of(floor_entity) == 5, 'got %s, want 2 + 1 + 2' % n_of(floor_entity))
        ck('a line without the reader\'s column count is not a row',
           n_of(floor_entity) == 5, 'the fixture carries a stray fragment')

        put('docs/entity-ratchet.tsv', '# every row gone\n')
        ck('an EMPTY ratchet baseline REFUSES, even though the other two '
           'populations would still sum to a plausible number',
           n_of(floor_entity) is None)

        # -- the declaration itself --------------------------------------
        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST,
            'ss_cpptest(alpha SOURCES a.cpp)\n'
            'ss_must_not_compile(g EXPECT "e" SOURCES g.cpp)\n')
        # Deliberately through the REAL stdout path, so quiet() has something
        # to hold back and the leak guard below is not asserting about an
        # empty list. A guard that passes on "nothing happened" is the shape
        # this whole file is against.
        quiet(declare, 'native')
        ck('a declaration is exactly the lines run-test-legs parses',
           transcripts[-1] == '[leg-floor] gtest-binaries == 1\n'
                              '[leg-floor] must-not-compile == 1\n'
                              '[leg-roster] gtest-binaries alpha\n'
                              '[leg-roster] must-not-compile g\n',
           repr(transcripts[-1]))

        # -- the roster ---------------------------------------------------
        put(NATIVE_MANIFEST,
            '# ss_cpptest(ghost SOURCES g.cpp) -- discussed, never built\n'
            'ss_cpptest(alpha SOURCES a.cpp)\n'
            'ss_cpptest_mathlib(beta SOURCES b.cpp)\n'
            'ss_must_not_compile(gamma EXPECT "error: no" SOURCES g.cpp)\n')
        names = roster_native()
        ck('the roster names every binary, not just how many',
           names == [('gtest-binaries', 'alpha'), ('gtest-binaries', 'beta'),
                     ('must-not-compile', 'gamma')], repr(names))
        ck('a binary that is only MENTIONED in a comment is not on the roster',
           not any(n == 'ghost' for _u, n in names))
        ck('the roster and the count are derived from the same lines',
           len([n for u, n in names if u == 'gtest-binaries'])
           == n_of(floor_native, 'gtest-binaries'))

        # A derivation that names a unit the runner cannot observe is refused
        # HERE as well as there. A floor nobody checks is a floor that cannot
        # fail, and a typo is the ordinary way to get one.
        LEGS['_bogus'] = lambda: [('gtests-binaries', '==', 3, 'typo')]
        try:
            declare('_bogus', out=io.StringIO())
            bad = False
        except CannotDerive:
            bad = True
        finally:
            del LEGS['_bogus']
        ck('a floor naming an unobservable unit is refused', bad)

    finally:
        ROOT = saved_root
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)

    # -- and the fixture is not the tree ---------------------------------
    #
    # Every check above ran against a stand-in. A stand-in drifts: rename
    # ss_cpptest tomorrow and all of them still pass while the real floor
    # silently becomes 0 -- which is the failure this file's own header calls
    # the worst one. So the last block asks the REAL tree, and asks for a
    # number big enough that a broken derivation cannot reach it by accident.
    for leg in sorted(LEGS):
        try:
            rows = LEGS[leg]()
            ns = ', '.join('%s %s %d' % (u, o, n) for u, o, n, _h in rows)
            # A tier leg's floors come from a measured list, where one binary
            # on one side of the line is a real answer; every other floor of 1
            # is the slack this test exists to catch.
            least = 1 if leg.endswith(('-fast', '-slow')) else 2
            ok = bool(rows) and all(n >= least for _u, _o, n, _h in rows)
        except CannotDerive as exc:
            ns, ok = str(exc), False
        ck('the REAL tree sizes the %s leg' % leg, ok, ns)

    for leg in sorted(ROSTERS):
        try:
            names = ROSTERS[leg]()
            # Only the units a roster names: the oracle leg also declares a
            # dotnet-tests floor, which is a population, not a roster.
            counts = dict((u, n) for u, _o, n, _h in LEGS[leg]()
                          if u in ('gtest-binaries', 'must-not-compile'))
            per = {}
            for u, _n in names:
                per[u] = per.get(u, 0) + 1
            ok = bool(names) and per == counts
            detail = '%d names: %s' % (len(names), per)
        except CannotDerive as exc:
            ok, detail = False, str(exc)
        ck('the REAL %s roster names as many as the floor counts' % leg,
           ok, detail)

    # The fixture derivations above emit `[leg-floor]` lines with deliberately
    # wrong numbers. If they ever reach stdout, run-test-legs reads them as
    # this leg's floor. tools/checkers pins the same property for the same
    # reason; this is that guard for this file.
    # `held` must be NON-ZERO for this to pass. If it is 0 the capture is not
    # capturing anything -- either quiet() stopped being used or the fixture
    # stopped declaring -- and "nothing leaked" would be true for the same
    # reason a scan of an empty tree is clean. Same shape as the guard in
    # tools/checkers, and it is worded as a floor for the same reason.
    held = sum(len(re.findall(r'^\[leg-floor\] ', t, re.M))
               for t in transcripts)
    checks += 1
    if held > 0:
        print('  ok    the fixture declarations were CAPTURED, not left on '
              'stdout   -- %d floor lines held back across %d passes'
              % (held, len(transcripts)))
    else:
        print('  FAIL  the fixture declarations were CAPTURED, not left on '
              'stdout   -- %d passes produced %d floor lines. If this is 0 '
              'the capture is not happening and run-test-legs will enforce '
              'the fixtures.' % (len(transcripts), held))
        failed += 1

    print('  ==> %d checks, %d failed' % (checks, failed))
    return 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    if '--self-test' in sys.argv[1:]:
        sys.exit(self_test())
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
